After a recent Windows 11 update where Microslop apparently made some Xinput changes, specifically to the xinput1_4.dll system dll. These updates affected several Nucleus Co-op handlers that use a xinput1_4.dll to restrict the controllers so if you are having issues with multiple controllers controlling all instances even with native Xinput controllers or handlers that use a xinput1_4.dll crashing when launched that is probably the reason why. If you haven’t updated yet consider holding off for now and if you already installed it, you could try rolling back the Windows update via system restore or uninstall it in Windows settings as a temporary workaround (though some users have reported this doesn't revert the Xinput changes sadly).We’re currently looking into the issue and will share more updates as soon as we know more.
